 =?utf-8?q?=E6=AD=A2=E7=B3=BB=E7=BB=9F=E7=95=8C=E9=9D=A2=E5=B4=A9=E6=BA=83?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An app can set its icon, logo or banner to an excessively large size
(e.g., 8,000 x 8,000 px). When loaded via PackageManager and drawn on
a hardware-accelerated canvas (e.g., in Settings or Launcher), this
throws a RuntimeException ("trying to draw too large bitmap") or
triggers an OOM, persistently crashing the system UI.

Here we are opening a new hidden api that gets resources but capped to
a maximum dimension of 2048 px. This api will be used by PackageManager
for fetching icon resources. Since it's not easy to determine if a
resource is icon/banner/logo just based on the id, we will be simply
relying on the callers to pass us that info.

In ResourcesImpl, we cache the capped resources with a separate key
(63rd bit of the key is used to mark capping).

